/* --- variable_arrays_rep / solver_arrays_rep: unfold on BOTH sides ----------
   The two bundles exist to keep the ANNOTATIONS readable; they are notation,
   not a reasoning step.  So each is erased on entry to the solver, on the left
   AND on the right, and every entailment is decided on the underlying atoms
   exactly as it was before the bundles existed.

   Unfolding only the left does not work.  A callee whose Require is folded has
   its `snap` existential occurring *nowhere but inside the bundle*, and QCP
   instantiates callee existentials by matching spatial atoms -- so once the
   caller's bundle has been shredded there is nothing left to unify `snap`
   against, and even a caller holding a syntactically identical bundle fails
   with "Cannot derive the precondition of function bcp".  Unfolding the right
   as well puts `snap` back inside `snap_states(?snap)` where the matcher can
   reach it.  This is the avl.strategies rule-6 idiom.

   A post-priority refold rule is deliberately absent: with both sides unfolded
   at core priority no bundle survives to post, so such a rule could never
   fire. */

id : 11
priority : core(1)
right : variable_arrays_rep(?vp, ?rp, ?lp, ?n, ?snap) at 0
action : right_erase(0);
         right_add(IntArray::full(vp, n, snap_values(snap)));
         right_add(IntArray::full(rp, n, snap_reasons(snap)));
         right_add(IntArray::full(lp, n, snap_levels(snap)));

id : 12
priority : core(1)
left : variable_arrays_rep(?vp, ?rp, ?lp, ?n, ?snap) at 0
action : left_erase(0);
         left_add(IntArray::full(vp, n, snap_values(snap)));
         left_add(IntArray::full(rp, n, snap_reasons(snap)));
         left_add(IntArray::full(lp, n, snap_levels(snap)));

